{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And Sarah laughed.</b> When Tishrei came after the tidings of the angels, and she saw with herself that she had not conceived, immediately she despaired of the tidings, for she had only six months until Passover, and she did not believe, as Abraham did, because Abraham heard from the mouth of HaKadosh Barukh Hu and therefore believed; but Sarah heard only from the mouth of the angels, and she supposed they were human beings. And she would look at her innards and say: is it possible that these bowels carry a child? Immediately, at that very moment, in Tishrei, 'and YHWH said to Abraham: why did Sarah laugh — at the appointed time I will return to you' — meaning, at the appointed time that the angel fixed, which is the coming Passover, 'I will return to you,' as He said, 'at the living season, and a son to Sarah.' And so this is the principal interpretation: she was informed twice. For we have a tradition that it was Passover when the angels came, as Rashi explained, and furthermore from the fact that it is written immediately regarding Lot, 'and matzot he baked' (i.e., unleavened bread); in the last Tishrei she laughed in her innards, as is proven in Rosh HaShanah, where it asks there: when does it refer to? As it says, 'at the appointed time I will return to you' — and it concludes that it refers to Tishrei. And furthermore, Rabbi Eliezer and Rabbi Yehoshua, who both say: on Rosh HaShanah Sarah, Rachel, and Hannah were remembered." "<b>Within her.</b> In her thought and heart." "<b>Saying.</b> According to its plain sense, 'saying' here is like every other 'saying' in Scripture — that is, she laughed and intended to say this utterance to people in a mocking manner, and therefore the verse was strict with her more than with Abraham." "<b>After I have grown old.</b> In wonderment — that is, how can it be that after I have grown old I will have now youthfulness, and moreover that my master is old? And this is the change: that HaKadosh Barukh Hu did not report all her words, and did not wish to say that she said <b>and my master is old</b>. And do not wonder at the word היתה, for in many places in Scripture we find past tense used for future and future used for past."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The reverent abbreviations הקב״ה and ה׳‎ are rendered as HaKadosh Barukh Hu and YHWH respectively, per voice rules for divine names and their standard expansions."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:04:46.444178531Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}